## Strongest Evidence

* Ford's essay *On Impressionism* provides the theoretical framework for understanding the novel's narrative method.
* Dowell repeatedly contradicts his own descriptions of Edward Ashburnham, revealing the instability of his judgements.
* Dowell's admissions of ignorance ("I don't know" and "I know nothing") expose the limits of his authority as narrator.
* The novel's disrupted chronology mirrors the workings of memory rather than objective time.
* The repeated description of the four protagonists as "good people" gradually collapses as Dowell uncovers their hidden lives. 

