## My Research

Although my research focuses on movement and embodiment in Virginia Woolf, this article is useful because it reframes movement as cognitive rather than physical. Dowell continually revises earlier impressions as new information emerges, suggesting that modernist narrative itself moves through acts of reinterpretation. This provides an interesting comparison with Woolf's mobile consciousness, where perception similarly unfolds through shifting impressions rather than stable truths.

## Critique

### Strengths

The article provides a clear introduction to Ford's Impressionist aesthetics and explains why Dowell has become one of modernism's classic unreliable narrators. It synthesises major strands of Ford criticism into an accessible overview and supports its claims with close textual analysis.

### Limitations

The article contributes little original theoretical insight beyond existing Ford scholarship. Much of the discussion summarises earlier critics rather than developing a new argument, and recent work in narratology and cognitive literary theory is largely absent. Consequently, it functions better as an introductory survey than as a major scholarly intervention.

